(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["landscape",{"c:the-ai-engineer-role":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An \u003cb>AI engineer\u003c/b> builds products on top of models someone else trained. The core work is \u003ci>adapting\u003c/i> a foundation model to a task (prompts, context, retrieval, tools, sometimes fine-tuning) and \u003ci>proving\u003c/i> it works (evals, monitoring), not designing and training models from scratch.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where the role came from\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Before 2022, putting machine learning into a product usually meant collecting labelled data, training a model and deploying it. That is \u003cb>ML engineering\u003c/b>. Large pretrained \"foundation models\" changed the starting point: a single model behind an API can already summarise, classify, extract, write code and chat. In 2023 swyx called the people who build on these models \u003cb>AI engineers\u003c/b>, sitting between ML researchers and full-stack developers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Three differences from ML engineering\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>ML engineering\u003c/th>\u003cth>AI engineering\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Starting point\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your data; you train a model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A pretrained model; you adapt it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Main levers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Features, architecture, training\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompts, context, retrieval, tools, fine-tuning\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model size and cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often small, cheap to run\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Large; inference cost and latency dominate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Evaluating output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed labels: accuracy, F1, AUC\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-ended text: hard to score, needs evals and judges\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Workflow\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data, then model, then product\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Product demo first, then data and models\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The three points to remember:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Adaptation, not training.\u003c/b> You rarely train weights. You change what the model sees (prompt and context) and what it can do (tools). Fine-tuning is a late, targeted step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inference is the expensive part.\u003c/b> Every request costs money and hundreds of milliseconds to seconds. Token budgets, caching and model choice are daily concerns, which is why this guide spends a whole module on billing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluation is the hard part.\u003c/b> A classifier is right or wrong. A paragraph of advice can be fluent, partly right and subtly harmful. Building evals that catch that is the core professional skill.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>How it differs from ordinary software engineering\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The components are \u003cb>probabilistic\u003c/b>. The same input can give different outputs, a prompt change can break cases you did not test, and a model update from your provider can shift behaviour overnight. So AI engineers borrow habits from data science: look at real outputs, measure on datasets, and roll out changes behind evals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the work looks like day to day\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Turning a vague product idea into a prompt, context and tool design\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reading traces of real conversations to find failure modes\u003c/li>\u003cli>Writing evals and running them on every change\u003c/li>\u003cli>Wiring retrieval over company data and tools over company APIs\u003c/li>\u003cli>Watching cost, latency and safety in production\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-tip\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Tip\u003c/p>\u003cp>You do not need to be a researcher. You do need to understand how models work well enough to predict their failures, which is what the rest of this stage gives you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Contrast AI engineering with ML engineering in three points.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(1) ML engineers train models on their own data; AI engineers adapt a pretrained foundation model with prompts, context, tools and occasionally fine-tuning. (2) AI engineering works with much larger models, so inference cost and latency become central design constraints. (3) Outputs are open-ended, so evaluation is harder and becomes the main engineering discipline. A fourth common point: the workflow is inverted, starting with a working product demo before investing in data and models.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a provider's model update matter more to an AI engineer than a library update matters to a web developer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Model behaviour is not specified by an interface contract. An update can change tone, format, refusals or accuracy on your cases without any API change, so only your evals will tell you whether it broke something.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two activities that take up much of an AI engineer's week.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: reading traces and doing error analysis, writing and running evals, designing prompts and context, wiring retrieval and tools, monitoring cost, latency and safety.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:landscape/the-modern-ai-application-stack":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Almost every AI product is the same stack: an \u003cb>app\u003c/b> sends a request through a \u003cb>gateway\u003c/b> to a \u003cb>model\u003c/b>, after \u003cb>context\u003c/b> (prompts, retrieved data, memory) has been assembled and \u003cb>tools\u003c/b> have been offered. \u003cb>Guardrails\u003c/b> check what goes in and out, and \u003cb>evals and observability\u003c/b> tell you whether it works. Learn the layers once and every new framework is just a different arrangement of them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A request, end to end\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User message\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Guardrails\u003c/b>\u003csmall>input checks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Context assembly\u003c/b>\u003csmall>prompt, memory, retrieved docs\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Gateway\u003c/b>\u003csmall>routing, caching, fallbacks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Tool calls?\u003c/b>\u003csmall>loop back with results\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Guardrails\u003c/b>\u003csmall>output checks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Response\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every arrow is logged as a trace, and every component is something you can improve independently.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The layers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical tools\u003c/th>\u003cth>Module in this guide\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generate text, code, images, speech\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude, GPT, Gemini, open-weight models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Foundations\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inference\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run models; batch and cache work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider APIs, vLLM, SGLang, Ollama\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inference, Serving\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Gateway\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One API for many models; routing, fallbacks, budgets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LiteLLM, OpenRouter, cloud gateways\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Routers, Reliability\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Decide what the model sees each turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompt templates, memory stores, compaction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Context, Memory\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Find relevant knowledge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Embeddings, vector DBs, BM25, rerankers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieval stage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Let the model act\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Function calling, MCP servers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tools, MCP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Orchestration\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Loops and workflows around model calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plain code, LangGraph, agent SDKs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agents stage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Guardrails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Block unsafe input and output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Classifiers, validators, policies\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Security\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Evals and observability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Measure quality, cost, latency; find failures\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Langfuse, Phoenix, Inspect, promptfoo\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Production stage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Build it up gradually\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Chip Huyen's advice, which matches what strong teams do: start with the simplest version (app calls model), then add one layer only when a real problem demands it. Add retrieval when the model lacks knowledge, a gateway when you use several models, caching when cost hurts, and agents only when a fixed workflow cannot do the job.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-warn\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Watch out\u003c/p>\u003cp>Frameworks often hide several layers behind one call. That is fine for a prototype, but when something breaks you must know which layer failed: wrong context, wrong tool result, model error or a guardrail false positive.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How this maps to Atlas\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Your portfolio project in this guide, \u003cb>Atlas\u003c/b>, follows exactly this path: a model client first, then cost tracking, prompts, tools, context and memory, retrieval, an agent loop and graph, MCP, evals, tracing, security and a gateway.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which layer would you add first if users complain the assistant does not know your company's policies?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Retrieval (and the context layer that places retrieved text in the prompt). The model lacks knowledge, not intelligence.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does a gateway give you that calling providers directly does not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One interface for many models plus routing, fallbacks when a provider fails, central logging, caching and spend limits.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why add layers one at a time?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each layer adds cost, latency and failure modes. Adding them only when a measured problem demands it keeps the system debuggable and shows whether each layer actually helps.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:foundation-models-and-what-llms-can-do":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>LLMs are excellent at transforming and generating language when the needed information is in front of them. They are unreliable at exact computation, facts they were never shown, and anything needing guaranteed correctness. Their ability is \u003cb>jagged\u003c/b>: a model can ace a hard task and fail a nearby easy one.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where LLMs shine\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Transforming text:\u003c/b> summarise, rewrite, translate, change tone, convert formats (prose to JSON, SQL to English).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Extracting and classifying:\u003c/b> pull fields from invoices, tag tickets, detect sentiment or intent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Writing and explaining code:\u003c/b> boilerplate, tests, refactors, explanations of unfamiliar code.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Answering from provided context:\u003c/b> questions over a document or retrieved passages.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Drafting and brainstorming:\u003c/b> first drafts, options, outlines.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Following a procedure with tools:\u003c/b> look something up, call an API, check the result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Where they struggle (and the fix)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Weakness\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003cth>Usual fix\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exact arithmetic, counting letters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Text is processed as tokens, not characters or numbers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Give a calculator or code tool\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recent or private facts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Knowledge is frozen at the training cutoff and never included your data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieval, web search tools\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Confident wrong answers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Training rewards plausible continuations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Grounding, citations, permission to say \"I don't know\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Long, exact procedures\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small errors compound over many steps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Break into steps, verify each, use code\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Same answer every time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sampling is random; providers update models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low temperature, evals, pinned model versions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The jagged frontier\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A 2023 field experiment with 758 consultants at Boston Consulting Group found that for tasks inside the model's capability, people using GPT-4 finished more tasks, faster and at higher quality. For a task just outside it, people using AI were \u003ci>less\u003c/i> likely to get the right answer than those working without it, because the model's confident output misled them. The lesson for engineers: you cannot guess where the edge is. You find it with examples and evals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">List five tasks an LLM does well and two it does badly.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Well: summarising, translating, extracting fields into JSON, classifying tickets, writing and explaining code, answering questions from supplied documents. Badly: exact arithmetic or letter counting without tools; facts after its training cutoff or about your private data; guaranteeing correctness over long multi-step procedures.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \"how many r's are in strawberry\" trip up models that can write working code?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model sees tokens such as \"str\", \"aw\", \"berry\", not individual letters, so counting characters requires reasoning about spelling it never directly observes. Code or a tool solves it trivially.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the practical meaning of the jagged frontier for a product team?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Capability does not degrade smoothly with difficulty, so you must test the actual tasks users will bring, including near neighbours of the ones that work, rather than assume.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask any chat model: (1) to summarise a long email, (2) to multiply two seven-digit numbers, (3) about a news event from last week. Check each answer. Then repeat (2) asking it to use code, if the tool is available.\u003c/p>","c:frontier-versus-open-weight-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Closed frontier models\u003c/b> (served only through their maker's API) usually lead on the hardest tasks and need no infrastructure. \u003cb>Open-weight models\u003c/b> (you can download the weights) give you control, privacy and often lower cost at scale, and the best of them trail the frontier by months rather than years. Most companies use both.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The two families\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Closed frontier models\u003c/b> come from labs such as Anthropic (Claude), OpenAI (GPT), Google (Gemini) and xAI (Grok). You call them over an API or through clouds such as AWS Bedrock, Google Vertex AI or Azure. You cannot see or change the weights.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Open-weight models\u003c/b> come from labs such as DeepSeek, Alibaba (Qwen), Moonshot (Kimi), Zhipu (GLM), Mistral, Meta (Llama) and OpenAI (gpt-oss). You can run them on your own hardware, fine-tune them, or rent them from inference providers like Together, Fireworks or Groq.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>Model names in this guide are examples as of late 2026. New versions ship every few months; the trade-offs below change much more slowly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Trade-offs\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Closed frontier API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Open-weight model\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Peak quality\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually highest on hardest tasks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Close on many tasks; gap of months\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Setup\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An API key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hardware or a hosting provider\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per token; no fixed cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per token from a host, or GPU cost if self-hosted; cheaper at high steady volume\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data control\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sent to the provider (check retention terms)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can stay entirely in your network\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Customisation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompting; limited fine-tuning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full fine-tuning, quantisation, distillation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Stability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider can deprecate or change models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You pin exact weights forever\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Operations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Serving, scaling, monitoring are on you\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Total versus active parameters\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Many large models are \u003cb>mixture-of-experts (MoE)\u003c/b>: each token only passes through a few \"expert\" sub-networks. A model card might say \"1.6T total, 49B active\". Total parameters set the memory you need to hold the model; active parameters set the compute per token, and so speed and cost. You will see why in the Inside an LLM module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How teams combine them\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A common pattern: a frontier model for the hardest step (planning, complex reasoning, final answers), a small or open model for high-volume simple steps (classification, extraction, routing), and an open model where data must not leave the company.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you compare two closed and two open models on quality, speed and price, and what pattern do you usually see?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shortlist with a comparison site such as Artificial Analysis (its quality index, output tokens per second and blended price per million tokens), then run the same 20 to 50 of your own examples through all four and record score, latency and cost per request. The usual pattern: the closed frontier models win on the hardest examples; the best open models come close on routine ones at a lower price per token when served by an inference provider; and speed depends as much on the host's hardware as on the model, so the same open model can be several times faster on one provider than another.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give three reasons a company might choose an open-weight model even if a closed model scores higher.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Data must stay inside its network; it needs to fine-tune deeply or pin exact weights; it has high, steady volume where self-hosting is cheaper; it wants to avoid dependence on one provider.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A model is \"230B total, 22B active\". Which number matters for GPU memory and which for speed?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Total parameters (230B) must fit in memory; active parameters (22B) determine compute per token, so they drive speed and cost per token.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the main operational cost of self-hosting that API users never see?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Running the serving stack: GPUs, scaling, batching, monitoring, upgrades and on-call, plus capacity you pay for even when idle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:reasoning-models-and-test-time-compute":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Reasoning models\u003c/b> are trained to think before answering: they generate a long hidden chain of reasoning, check and revise it, then reply. Spending more computation at answer time (\u003cb>test-time compute\u003c/b>) buys accuracy on hard multi-step problems, at the price of more latency and more billed tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What changes inside\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A standard chat model starts answering immediately. A reasoning model first produces \u003cb>reasoning tokens\u003c/b> (also called thinking tokens): trying approaches, catching mistakes, backtracking. These are usually hidden or summarised in the API response, but you \u003cb>pay for them as output tokens\u003c/b> and wait for them.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Question\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Thinking\u003c/b>\u003csmall>hundreds to thousands of hidden tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Answer\u003c/b>\u003csmall>short visible output\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Labs taught models to do this with \u003cb>reinforcement learning on verifiable problems\u003c/b> (maths with checkable answers, code with tests), covered in the training module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The dial: effort or thinking budget\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Most APIs let you choose how hard the model thinks, as an \u003cb>effort level\u003c/b> (for example low, medium, high) or a \u003cb>maximum number of thinking tokens\u003c/b>. Higher effort means better answers on hard tasks, longer waits and bigger bills. On easy tasks it often adds cost without benefit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use which\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Use a reasoning model or high effort\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use a fast model or low effort\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-step maths, logic, planning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Classification, extraction, formatting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Debugging, complex code changes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short answers from given context\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Agent steps that decide what to do next\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High-volume, latency-sensitive calls\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Problems where a wrong answer is costly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Chat replies where speed matters most\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Prompting them differently\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reasoning models do not need \"think step by step\" instructions; they already do. Give a clear goal, constraints and the success criteria, and let them plan. Over-specifying every step can make results worse.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What thinking costs: a worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take one hard question with a 1,000-token prompt and a 300-token visible answer. Illustrative prices: $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens (thinking tokens are billed as output).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Fast mode (no thinking):\n  1,000 × $2 / 1M  +  300 × $10 / 1M            = $0.002 + $0.003 = $0.005\n\nHigh effort (4,000 thinking tokens + 300 answer):\n  1,000 × $2 / 1M  +  4,300 × $10 / 1M          = $0.002 + $0.043 = $0.045\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Nine times the price, and if the model generates about 60 tokens per second, roughly a minute of extra waiting before the answer starts. That is a bargain for a contract review that would otherwise be wrong, and a waste for sorting support tickets. Check the usage object of every response: it reports thinking or reasoning tokens separately, so you can see exactly what each effort level costs on your own tasks.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reasoning inside agents\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In agent loops, reasoning models can think \u003ci>between\u003c/i> tool calls (often called interleaved thinking): read a tool result, reconsider the plan, then choose the next call. That is a big part of why modern coding and research agents work. Two practical consequences:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Thinking happens on every step, so an agent's cost multiplies with both the number of steps and the effort level. Many teams use high effort for planning steps and low effort for routine ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Some providers require you to pass earlier thinking blocks back unchanged in the conversation, or the model loses its train of thought (the APIs module shows the message formats).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Other ways to buy accuracy with compute\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Thinking longer is one form of test-time compute. Others you can apply to any model: sample several answers and take the majority (self-consistency), generate candidates and keep the one a checker or test suite accepts, or have a second call critique and revise the first. The training module explains why these work.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-warn\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Watch out\u003c/p>\u003cp>The visible reasoning summary is not a faithful log of how the answer was produced. Do not rely on it as an explanation for audits; verify the answer instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run the same hard problem at low and high reasoning effort. What should you compare, and what do you typically find?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compare correctness, time to first visible token, total time, and the reasoning and output token counts in the usage object (which drive cost). Typically high effort is right more often on genuinely multi-step problems but takes several times longer and costs several times more; on easy problems both settings are right and low effort is simply cheaper.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a reasoning model cost several times more than a fast model for the same visible answer length?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its hidden reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens, and there can be thousands of them before a short answer. In the worked example, 4,000 thinking tokens turned a $0.005 request into a $0.045 one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two tasks where you would turn reasoning effort down.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any simple, high-volume or latency-sensitive task, such as classifying tickets, extracting fields or reformatting text.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is test-time compute scaling?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Improving answers by spending more computation at inference, through longer reasoning, sampling several attempts or verifying candidates, instead of only training a bigger model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Give a fast model and a reasoning model (or one model at low and high effort) the same tricky logic puzzle. Compare correctness, time to answer and the token usage reported in the response.\u003c/p>","c:small-and-on-device-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Small models (roughly 1 to 30 billion parameters) are fast, cheap and can run on a laptop, a phone or your own server. They handle narrow, well-defined jobs well and are the backbone of routing, classification and private or offline features.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why small models matter\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost and speed:\u003c/b> often ten to a hundred times cheaper per token than frontier models, with much lower latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Privacy:\u003c/b> data never leaves the device or your network.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Offline and edge:\u003c/b> works without internet, on phones and laptops.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fine-tuning:\u003c/b> small enough to customise on one GPU (see the fine-tuning module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How much hardware?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A rule of thumb for the weights alone:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>memory (GB) ≈ parameters (billions) × bytes per parameter\n8B model at 16-bit: 8 × 2   = 16 GB\n8B model at 4-bit:  8 × 0.5 ≈ 4-5 GB (plus overhead)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>You also need room for the KV cache, which grows with context length (next modules). \u003cb>Quantisation\u003c/b> stores weights in fewer bits; 4-bit versions lose a little quality but fit on ordinary laptops.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model size\u003c/th>\u003cth>16-bit weights\u003c/th>\u003cth>8-bit\u003c/th>\u003cth>4-bit\u003c/th>\u003cth>Runs comfortably on\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 2 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Phones, any laptop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 5 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Laptop with 16 GB RAM or a small GPU\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>14B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>28 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 8 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Laptop with 32 GB RAM, 12 to 16 GB GPU\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32B\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 18 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>24 GB GPU or 48 GB+ Apple silicon\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Add 10 to 30% for the runtime and a few gigabytes for a long context. On Apple silicon and other unified-memory machines, the GPU can use most of system RAM, which is why Macs are popular for local models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Calling a local model from code\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Local servers speak the same OpenAI-compatible format as cloud APIs, so switching is a base-URL change:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")  # key is ignored locally\nstart = time.perf_counter()\nfirst = None\nstream = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"&lt;local-model&gt;\", stream=True,\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise the benefits of unit tests in 100 words.\"}])\nchunks = 0\nfor chunk in stream:\n    if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n        first = first or time.perf_counter()\n        chunks += 1\nend = time.perf_counter()\nprint(f\"time to first token {first - start:.2f}s, ~{chunks / (end - first):.0f} chunks/s\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each streamed chunk is usually one token, so chunks per second is a fair estimate of generation speed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Tools to run them\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ollama\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One-command local models with an OpenAI-compatible API\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LM Studio\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Desktop app with a chat UI and local server\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>llama.cpp\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The engine underneath many tools; runs GGUF files on CPU and GPU\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MLX\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast inference on Apple silicon\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>vLLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Serving on a GPU server for many users (Serving module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Good and bad fits\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Good: classification, extraction, routing, autocomplete, summarising short text, embeddings, simple chat. Weaker: long multi-step reasoning, complex agentic tasks, broad world knowledge. A common design is a \u003cb>cascade\u003c/b>: try the small model, escalate to a large one when confidence is low.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You run a small model locally and time it. What should you measure, and what does each number tell you?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Measure time to first token (how long the prompt takes to process, which grows with prompt length), tokens per second during generation (how fast text appears, limited mostly by memory bandwidth), total time for a realistic task, and memory used. On a modern laptop a 4-bit 8B model typically generates tens of tokens per second, fast enough for interactive use; if it is much slower, the model probably did not fit in GPU or unified memory and spilled to the CPU.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly how much memory do the weights of a 14B model need at 4-bit?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 14 × 0.5 = 7 GB, plus overhead and KV cache, so plan for around 9 to 10 GB.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two product features that suit a small local model.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: on-device autocomplete, private document classification, offline summarisation, request routing, PII detection, embeddings for local search.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a cascade?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sending each request to a cheap model first and escalating to a larger model only when the small one is unsure or fails a check.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Install Ollama, run a 3 to 8B model (\u003ccode>ollama run &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>), and time a 200-word summary. Then call the same model from Python through its OpenAI-compatible endpoint at \u003ccode>http://localhost:11434/v1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>","c:benchmarks-and-their-limits":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A benchmark is a fixed set of questions with a scoring rule. Scores are useful for shortlisting models, but they stop telling models apart once everyone scores near the top (\u003cb>saturation\u003c/b>), and they overstate ability when test questions leaked into training data (\u003cb>contamination\u003c/b>). Your own eval on your own task is what decides.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Benchmarks you will see in launch posts\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Area\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\u003cth>Measures\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Knowledge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MMLU, MMLU-Pro\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multiple-choice questions across 57+ subjects\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Expert reasoning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GPQA Diamond, Humanity's Last Exam\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Very hard questions written by domain experts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Maths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GSM8K, AIME, FrontierMath\u003c/td>\u003ctd>From grade-school word problems to research maths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Coding and agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SWE-bench Verified, Terminal-Bench\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixing real GitHub issues; working in a terminal\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Abstraction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>ARC-AGI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Novel visual puzzles\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human preference\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LMArena\u003c/td>\u003ctd>People vote between two anonymous answers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Saturation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A benchmark is \u003cb>saturated\u003c/b> when top models score so close to the maximum that differences are within noise, so it no longer separates models.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-example\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Example\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>MMLU.\u003c/b> When it was released in 2020, GPT-3 scored about 44%. By 2024, frontier models scored around 90%. A 2024 re-annotation study (\"Are we done with MMLU?\") estimated that about 6.5% of MMLU questions contain errors, so the true ceiling sits below 100% and a one-point difference between two models means almost nothing. The field moved to harder sets such as MMLU-Pro, GPQA Diamond and Humanity's Last Exam, which are now themselves climbing. GSM8K maths followed the same path.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Contamination\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A benchmark is \u003cb>contaminated\u003c/b> when its questions, answers or close paraphrases appeared in a model's training data. The model may then recall rather than reason, and its score overstates real ability. Because models train on huge web scrapes, and benchmarks live on the web, this is hard to avoid.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-example\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Example\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>GSM8K versus GSM1k.\u003c/b> In 2024, researchers at Scale AI wrote 1,250 new grade-school maths problems matched to GSM8K in style and difficulty but never published. Some model families scored up to 8 percentage points lower on the fresh set, and models that were more likely to reproduce GSM8K text verbatim showed bigger gaps, which points to partial memorisation. Most frontier models showed little drop.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cp>How the field fights contamination: private held-out test sets, benchmarks refreshed with new questions after model cutoffs (for example LiveBench and LiveCodeBench), canary strings that let labs filter test data from training, and evaluating on problems published after a model's training cutoff.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Other limits\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Harness sensitivity.\u003c/b> Agent benchmarks such as SWE-bench measure the model \u003ci>plus\u003c/i> its scaffolding. The same model can score very differently with different tools and prompts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompt and format sensitivity.\u003c/b> Small changes in wording, answer format or number of examples move scores by several points.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Goodhart's law.\u003c/b> When a score becomes the target, labs tune for it, and it stops measuring general ability.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Preference leaderboards\u003c/b> reward style as well as substance: longer, well-formatted answers tend to win votes. A 2025 study, \"The Leaderboard Illusion\", also argued that private pre-release testing let some providers present only their best variants.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Not your task.\u003c/b> No public benchmark contains your users, your documents or your definition of a good answer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How professionals use benchmarks\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Use leaderboards to \u003cb>shortlist\u003c/b> three to five candidate models.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prefer recent, hard, contamination-resistant benchmarks close to your task (coding agents: SWE-bench Verified; expert Q&amp;A: GPQA).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decide with your own eval\u003c/b> of 30 to 100 real examples, measuring quality, latency and cost.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain saturation and contamination with one example each.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Saturation: top models score near the ceiling so the benchmark no longer separates them; MMLU went from about 44% (GPT-3, 2020) to about 90% by 2024, with around 6.5% of its questions estimated to be wrong, so small differences are noise. Contamination: test questions leak into training data so scores reflect memory; on GSM1k, a fresh set mirroring GSM8K, some model families dropped by up to 8 points, suggesting they had partly memorised GSM8K.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a model's SWE-bench score differ between two reports?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The score depends on the agent harness (tools, prompts, number of attempts) as well as the model, and on which subset (for example Verified) was used.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two ways to reduce contamination risk in an evaluation.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep a private held-out test set; use questions created after the model's training cutoff; refresh questions regularly; use canary strings.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A model tops a preference leaderboard. What should you still check?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whether it wins on your task under your eval, its cost and latency, and whether its lead comes from style (length, formatting) rather than correctness.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:choosing-a-model-for-a-task":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pick the \u003cb>cheapest, fastest model that clears your quality bar on your own examples\u003c/b>, within your constraints on latency, context, data and licence. Write the criteria first, shortlist from leaderboards, then decide with a 30 to 100 example eval.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The process\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Define task and quality bar\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>List constraints\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Shortlist 3-5 models\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Run your eval\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Compare quality, latency, cost\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Pick and wrap behind an interface\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Criteria that matter\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Criterion\u003c/th>\u003cth>Question to ask\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Quality\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does it pass my eval cases? Which failures remain?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Time to first token and total time, at p50 and p95\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cost per 1,000 requests at my real token counts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does my longest realistic input fit, with room to spare?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Features\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool calling, structured output, vision, caching, batch\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retention, region, zero-data-retention options, compliance\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Licence and lock-in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commercial terms; can I switch later?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example: a customer-support bot\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Requirements: answer from a help centre, 95% of answers grounded in retrieved articles, first token under 1.5 seconds, under $0.01 per conversation, EU data residency.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Cost per request, with prices per million tokens:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cost = (input_tokens × input_price + output_tokens × output_price) / 1,000,000\n\nExample: 3,000 input tokens, 300 output tokens,\nmodel at $1.00 in / $5.00 out per million:\n(3,000 × 1.00 + 300 × 5.00) / 1,000,000 = $0.0045 per request\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>You run 50 real support questions through four shortlisted models, score groundedness and correctness, and record latency and cost. The mid-sized model passes the bar at a third of the flagship's cost, so you choose it and route only escalations to the flagship.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A 30-example eval in about 40 lines\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The decision needs evidence, and the evidence can be small. Keep examples in a JSONL file and score them with the simplest check that works:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, time, statistics\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=GATEWAY_URL, api_key=KEY)          # one endpoint, many models\nPRICES = {\"&lt;model-a&gt;\": (1.0, 5.0), \"&lt;model-b&gt;\": (3.0, 15.0)}  # $ per 1M input, output tokens\ncases = [json.loads(line) for line in open(\"cases.jsonl\")]   # {\"question\": ..., \"must_contain\": [...]}\n\ndef passed(answer, case):\n    return all(term.lower() in answer.lower() for term in case[\"must_contain\"])\n\nfor model, (p_in, p_out) in PRICES.items():\n    scores, latencies, costs = [], [], []\n    for case in cases:\n        t = time.perf_counter()\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, temperature=0,\n            messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM_PROMPT},\n                      {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": case[\"question\"]}])\n        latencies.append(time.perf_counter() - t)\n        scores.append(passed(r.choices[0].message.content, case))\n        costs.append((r.usage.prompt_tokens * p_in + r.usage.completion_tokens * p_out) / 1e6)\n    p95 = sorted(latencies)[int(0.95 * (len(latencies) - 1))]\n    print(f\"{model}: pass {sum(scores)}/{len(cases)}, p50 {statistics.median(latencies):.1f}s, \"\n          f\"p95 {p95:.1f}s, ${sum(costs) / len(cases) * 1000:.2f} per 1,000 requests\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Keyword checks are crude but fast; the Evals module shows better scorers, including LLM judges. The output becomes a scorecard like this (illustrative numbers):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Pass rate\u003c/th>\u003cth>p50 latency\u003c/th>\u003cth>p95 latency\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost per 1,000 requests\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Flagship\u003c/td>\u003ctd>29/30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.1 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.0 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$13.50\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mid-size\u003c/td>\u003ctd>28/30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.2 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.2 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$4.50\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Small\u003c/td>\u003ctd>22/30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.6 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.1 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.60\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Open-weight via host\u003c/td>\u003ctd>27/30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.9 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.9 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.80\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Read the failures, not just the counts: two misses that are both \"refused a valid question\" call for a prompt fix, while misses on multi-step questions suggest the task needs a stronger model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to re-run it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Re-run the eval when a provider ships a new model or deprecates yours, when your prompt or retrieval changes, when prices move, and on a schedule (quarterly is common). Because the eval is a script, a re-decision takes an afternoon instead of a debate.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-tip\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Tip\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep every model behind a small interface (\u003ccode>generate(messages, **options)\u003c/code>). New models ship monthly; you want switching to be a config change plus an eval run.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Write three selection criteria for a support-bot use case.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For example: answers grounded in the help centre on at least 95% of eval questions; first token under 1.5 seconds at p95; total cost under one cent per conversation; data processed in the EU; supports tool calling for order lookup.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why choose the cheapest model that passes rather than the best model available?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Above the quality bar, extra capability does not help users, while cost and latency scale with every request. You can still escalate hard cases to a larger model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A model is $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens. What does a request with 2,000 input and 500 output tokens cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(2,000 × 3 + 500 × 15) / 1,000,000 = (6,000 + 7,500) / 1,000,000 = $0.0135.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:model-routers-and-multi-provider-access":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The OpenAI chat format became the common language of model APIs. \u003cb>Gateways\u003c/b> and \u003cb>routers\u003c/b> use it to give you one interface to many models, with fallbacks, load balancing, spend limits and central logging. Switching models becomes a string change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three ways to reach many models\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hosted router (OpenRouter)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One key and bill for hundreds of models from many providers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Experiments, quick comparisons\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Library or proxy (LiteLLM)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Translate one call format to every provider; optional self-hosted proxy with keys, budgets, logs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teams that want control\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cloud platform (Bedrock, Vertex AI, Azure AI Foundry)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many models inside your cloud account and network\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Enterprise data and billing rules\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The pattern in code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\", api_key=OPENROUTER_KEY)\nfor model in [\"anthropic/&lt;model&gt;\", \"openai/&lt;model&gt;\", \"qwen/&lt;model&gt;\"]:\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=model,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain recursion in one sentence.\"}],\n    )\n    print(model, r.choices[0].message.content, r.usage)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Local servers (Ollama, vLLM) expose the same format, so the same code can hit a model on your laptop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What gateways add in production\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fallbacks:\u003c/b> if provider A errors or times out, retry on provider B.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Routing:\u003c/b> send easy requests to a cheap model, hard ones to a strong model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budgets and rate limits\u003c/b> per team, user or key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Central logging\u003c/b> of tokens, cost and latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caching\u003c/b> of repeated requests.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Fallbacks in a few lines\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With LiteLLM's router you declare model groups once and say what to try when one fails:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from litellm import Router\n\nrouter = Router(\n    model_list=[\n        {\"model_name\": \"main\",   \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"anthropic/&lt;model&gt;\"}},\n        {\"model_name\": \"backup\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"openai/&lt;model&gt;\"}},\n    ],\n    fallbacks=[{\"main\": [\"backup\"]}],   # on errors or timeouts, retry the request on \"backup\"\n    num_retries=2,\n    timeout=30,\n)\nresponse = router.completion(model=\"main\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello\"}])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Your application only ever asks for \u003ccode>\"main\"\u003c/code>; which provider answered is a logging detail. The same idea exists in hosted routers (a list of fallback models per request) and in cloud gateways.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Routing strategies\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it decides\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Static by task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each feature is pinned to a model in config\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most products; simple and predictable\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Classifier router\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A cheap model or rules label each request easy or hard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High volume with a mix of difficulties\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cascade\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Try the cheap model; escalate if a check fails or confidence is low\u003c/td>\u003ctd>When you can verify answers (tests, schemas)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost or latency aware\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pick the cheapest or fastest provider serving the same model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-weight models offered by several hosts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Start with static routing. Add a classifier or cascade only when your cost data shows that a large share of traffic could be handled by a cheaper model without failing your eval (the Reliability module builds one).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-warn\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Watch out\u003c/p>\u003cp>A common format hides real differences. Provider-specific features (prompt-cache controls, thinking settings, citations, some tool types) may be dropped or translated imperfectly. Test the features you rely on through the gateway, and check the gateway's own data-retention policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you call three models through one OpenAI-compatible endpoint?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Create one OpenAI client whose \u003ccode>base_url\u003c/code> points at the gateway (for example OpenRouter or a LiteLLM proxy) with the gateway's key, then send the same \u003ccode>chat.completions.create\u003c/code> call three times, changing only the \u003ccode>model\u003c/code> string (such as provider/model names). The messages, parameters and response parsing stay identical, and \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> in each response gives you tokens for cost comparison.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can the same Python code call a cloud model and a local Ollama model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Both expose an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint, so only the base URL, key and model name change.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two production features of a gateway beyond a unified API.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: fallbacks across providers, routing by difficulty or cost, per-user budgets and rate limits, central logging of cost and latency, response caching.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a risk of relying on a hosted router for sensitive data?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is another party that sees your prompts; you must check its retention and privacy terms in addition to the model provider's.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:open-weight-licences-and-the-open-source-ai-definition":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\"Open\" models come with very different rights. \u003cb>Open weights\u003c/b> means you can download the parameters, but the licence may restrict use. \u003cb>Open source AI\u003c/b>, as defined by the Open Source Initiative, also requires the code and enough information about the training data to rebuild the model. Always read the licence before you ship.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The spectrum\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\u003cth>What you get\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples of licence style\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Closed API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Access only; provider terms apply\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most frontier models\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Open weights, custom licence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights with conditions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama community licences (a monthly-active-user threshold, acceptable-use policy, attribution)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Open weights, permissive licence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights under Apache 2.0 or MIT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many Qwen, Mistral, DeepSeek and gpt-oss releases\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fully open\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights, training code and data details\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Research models such as OLMo\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What to check in a model licence\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Commercial use\u003c/b> allowed? Any user or revenue thresholds?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Acceptable-use policy\u003c/b> that forbids your domain?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Using outputs to train other models\u003c/b>: allowed or forbidden?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Attribution and naming\u003c/b> rules for fine-tunes and products.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Redistribution\u003c/b> of modified weights.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data provenance\u003c/b>: do you need to know what the model was trained on for compliance?\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>A worked check: two models, two outcomes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Model under Apache 2.0\u003c/th>\u003cth>Model under the Llama 4 community licence\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Commercial use?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, unless your products had more than 700 million monthly active users on the release date; then you must ask Meta for a licence\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Conditions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep the licence and notices; state changes you made\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ship a copy of the agreement; display \"Built with Llama\"; follow Meta's acceptable-use policy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fine-tunes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Name them as you like\u003c/td>\u003ctd>If you distribute a model trained or improved with Llama or its outputs, its name must start with \"Llama\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Patent grant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, explicit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not the same as Apache's; read the terms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Verdict for a startup's SaaS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Use freely\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usable, with attribution, naming and policy obligations to track\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The pattern generalises: permissive licences (Apache 2.0, MIT) impose notice requirements only; custom \"community\" licences add thresholds, attribution, naming and use policies; some research releases are non-commercial only (for example CC BY-NC), which rules out paid products entirely.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Licences on data and outputs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model licence is not the only one. Fine-tuning datasets have their own licences (many are non-commercial or share-alike), and some licences and API terms restrict using a model's \u003ci>outputs\u003c/i> to train a competing model. If you plan to distil a large model into a small one (fine-tuning module), check both the teacher's terms and the dataset licences first.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Do not forget API terms\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For closed models the equivalent questions are about data: is your data used for training (usually not for business APIs, but check), how long is it retained, is zero-data-retention available, and in which regions is it processed?\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-tip\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Tip\u003c/p>\u003cp>Licences change between model versions from the same lab. Check the licence on the exact model card you deploy, and record it in your project's documentation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Check the licence of two open models for commercial use. What do you look at, and what might the two outcomes be?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Open each model card, find the licence file, and check commercial-use permission, user or revenue thresholds, acceptable-use policy, attribution and naming rules, output-use restrictions and redistribution terms. A model under Apache 2.0 can be used commercially with only notice requirements. A model under the Llama 4 community licence can be used commercially too, but you must ask Meta for a licence above 700 million monthly active users, display \"Built with Llama\", name distributed derivatives starting with \"Llama\" and follow the acceptable-use policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between open weights and open source AI?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Open weights only means the parameters are downloadable, possibly under restrictive terms. The OSI's open source AI definition also requires the freedom to use, study, modify and share, with the code and sufficient training-data information to rebuild the system.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three clauses you would look for before shipping an open-weight model commercially.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Commercial-use permission and any user thresholds; acceptable-use restrictions; rules on using outputs to train other models; attribution or naming requirements; redistribution terms.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your company forbids sending customer data to third parties. Which option fits?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A self-hosted open-weight model with a licence that permits your commercial use, or a provider deployment inside your own cloud account with contractual guarantees.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:landscape/same-prompt-five-models":"\u003cp>You will send the same five prompts to five models and record what you see. The point is to build intuition you cannot get from leaderboards.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+, \u003ccode>pip install openai\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>An OpenRouter key (or two provider keys) with a spending limit of a few dollars\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ollama with one small model pulled for the local run\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Pick five models: two closed frontier or mid-size models, two open-weight models, one local model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write five prompts covering different skills:\u003cul>\u003cli>Extract name, date and amount as JSON from a messy email\u003c/li>\u003cli>A logic puzzle with a single correct answer\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a Python function with an edge case (for example, merge overlapping intervals)\u003c/li>\u003cli>Summarise a 600-word article in three bullet points\u003c/li>\u003cli>A question about something that happened last month\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run every prompt on every model and record output, latency and token usage:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time, json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nMODELS = {\"&lt;closed-1&gt;\": \"openrouter\", \"&lt;closed-2&gt;\": \"openrouter\", \"&lt;open-1&gt;\": \"openrouter\",\n          \"&lt;open-2&gt;\": \"openrouter\", \"&lt;local&gt;\": \"ollama\"}\nclients = {\"openrouter\": OpenAI(base_url=\"https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\", api_key=KEY),\n           \"ollama\": OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")}\nrows = []\nfor model, host in MODELS.items():\n    for name, prompt in PROMPTS.items():\n        t = time.perf_counter()\n        r = clients[host].chat.completions.create(model=model, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}])\n        rows.append(dict(model=model, prompt=name, seconds=round(time.perf_counter() - t, 2),\n                         tokens_in=r.usage.prompt_tokens, tokens_out=r.usage.completion_tokens,\n                         text=r.choices[0].message.content))\njson.dump(rows, open(\"results.json\", \"w\"), indent=2)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Score each answer 1 to 5 by hand and add a one-line note on any failure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make a table: model by prompt, with score, seconds and estimated cost.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>All models handle the summary; differences show on the puzzle, the code edge case and the JSON.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The local model is fastest to start but weakest on the puzzle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>No model knows last month's news without a search tool, but some say so and some invent an answer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which prompt separated the models most, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Usually the logic puzzle or the edge-case code, because they need multi-step reasoning; summarisation is easy for all of them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:model-selection-scorecard":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Pick a real task from your work or a realistic one (support answers, ticket classification, invoice extraction, SQL generation). Produce a one-page recommendation backed by your own measurements. This becomes the template you reuse every time a new model ships.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>30 or more real or realistic examples with expected outputs or grading notes\u003c/li>\u003cli>Four candidate models: two closed, two open-weight (one may be local)\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each model: quality score, p50 and p95 latency, cost per 1,000 requests\u003c/li>\u003cli>Automatic scoring where possible (exact match, JSON validity, tests), manual scoring for the rest\u003c/li>\u003cli>A script that re-runs the whole comparison with one command\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Write the task definition and the quality bar in two or three sentences.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the example set as JSONL: \u003ccode>{\"input\": ..., \"expected\": ..., \"notes\": ...}\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a runner that calls each model through one client and stores outputs, tokens and timings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the scorers; hand-grade where code cannot.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute the scorecard table and a short failure analysis per model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the recommendation: chosen model, runner-up, when to re-evaluate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python run.py\u003c/code> regenerates the scorecard from scratch\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report shows quality, latency and cost side by side, with at least three concrete failure examples\u003c/li>\u003cli>The recommendation names the cheapest model that clears the bar, or explains why none does\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add a cascade: small model first, escalate on low confidence; measure the blended cost and quality\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run each example three times to measure output variance\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a chart of quality against cost per request\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
